I don't really remember encountering any problems telling the difference
between those two items.  Usually, the differences between the two are
fairly obvious - doors usually stand out on the edge of a room, and
spellbooks are usually a different colour than the door.

Personally, I don't have anything against tiles - however, the Windows
version of the game doesn't have a message bar that is as readable as it
could be.  (The game is white/colour-on-black, making it hard to read the
black-on-white messages.)  


>People always said that the graphical version looked silly, that their 
>imaginations made, for example, a group of L much more frightening than any 
>tile could look, etc. What that ignores is that after some time of play, the 
>player isn't really paying attention to the symbol/tile any more at all. 
>He's seeing *through* the screen and into the game itself. It doesn't matter 
>that characters look in the wrong direction and arrows fly sideways. You're 
>focused on playing the game; the interface is just a means to that end.

It might not matter that arrows fly sideways, but they certainly are
distracting.  The fix is to create more tiles.

>
>So far as I am concerned, the advantages of ascii are knowing intuitively 
>what species go together for blessed genocide, and a certain "retro" 
>quality. The advantage of tiles is a more intuitive interface. And that's 
>it. Everything else regarding interface preference is strictly habitual.

Actually, the advantages aren't really that significant.

The ability to identify which monster class a monster belongs to with a
blessed genocide scroll is an interface limitation - modifing the code to
allow displaying the list of species will help users identify the greatest
potential threats without having to worry about the actual character.

The inuitiveness of tiles isn't much of a benefit either - pressing '/' and
moving the cursor to the target will show you what a specific creature is.
The same applies to tiles, if you find them to be less intuitive.  

